Steven@uicadd.csl.uiuc.edu (Steven Parkes) writes:
>
> I'm in the process of purchasing more memory for our 5000.  I already have
> calls into DataRam, Standard Memories, and Camintonn.  If there are any
> other sources, I'd apprciate hearing about them.  Also, it seems that the
> simms for the 5000 are unique (i.e. not generic / can't be bought from the
> people who sell jelly-bean simms) -- can anyone confirm or deny this?

Another vendor I'm aware of is ClearPoint.  Don't know access info -
should be in multiple trade rags (they also do SIMMs for alot of
others).  ClearPoint also does 4Mbit DRAM based SIMMs, allowing the
DS5000 to reach 480MB.  Digital will eventually have 4Mbit DRAM SIMMs
also.  I don't know the cost/MB breakdown of 1Mbit vs. 4Mbit.  Some of
the DS5000s in our Western Research Labs run at 480MB using
third-party SIMMs.  ULTRIX supports up to 512MB, the 480MB ceiling is
in the way the DS5000 has the TURBOchannel I/O space.


Re: "generic" SIMMs.   There actually isn't any such thing.  The
volume of PC SIMMs is high enough that many people consider them a
"standard", but there is no formal or even de facto standard for
workstation SIMMs.  The commodity nature of PC SIMMs simply make them
appear that way. ("jelly-bean" SIMMs)

One of the prime factors that make fast workstations fast is a very
quick memory subsystem (and a large, fast and smart cache).  This
often means that the memory SIMMs have to be tailored to a platform,
although some commonality within a vendor's family of machines should
be expected.

In the case of the DS5000 SIMMs, they are ECC, not parity, while most
lower-performance workstations (and all PCs, as far as I know) are
parity (or worse, with some older PCs).  The ECC is required due to
the huge number of parts required to reach the large MB ceilings -
statistics tells us that parity isn't good enough when you have that
many chips - you'll be getting memory errors too often.

I'd be suspicious of any workstation that uses PC SIMMs and claims to
deliver very high CPU performance.  Chances are good that their claims
are based on trivial benchmarks that pretty much stay in the cache.
Run a real application (or multi-task the small ones) and the slow
memory subsystem puts quite a drag on the system.

I'd be interested in seeing what you've learned from the various
vendors; i.e., price, size, price/MB, 1 or 4 Mbit, warranty...

