      Use the 'rm' field in /etc/printcap or use the lprsetup command, and
      use the same entry for all clients.
      For example:
      # @(#)printcap	2.2 (ULTRIX) 4/12/89
      lw50|ps|psc|lw|postscript printer in 50 :\
                :lp=:\
                :mx#0:\
                :rm=remote_hostname:\
                :rp=lw50:\
                :sd=/usr/spool/lpd/lw50:


|> (3) Will a client (16mb) function as a print spool server reliably without
|> effecting the performance too much.

      I should think so.  Lpd is pretty low overhead unless it's going 
      constantly.
 
