We went through a similar exercise here about 6 months ago.
Our disk space requirement was not so high but we needed about 3GB.
We had 11/785 + 5*UVAX II/2000 in a cluster so our upgrade was
much more easily defined. We halved our number of machines,
increased disk space and processor power while cutting costs.

Have you thought about using a LAVC? Even to stick to DEC
hardware, if you got a couple of VS3100-76 with 2xRZ57 each,
plus an RZ24 as system disk each, unless your I/O overheads
are massive, then you would have a config that beats the pants
off an 8650! 

There is no reason why you couldn't poke the internal SCSI bus out
the back somehow, it would purely be a matter of connecting the cables.
This would be a better solution, because it's probably not a good
idea to have more than 4 or 5 devices on the SCSI bus for performance
reasons. But seriously, an LAVC is a pretty good solution to
the problem.

Also, a TZ30 is connected to the internal SCSI bus.
