In article <1991Jan14.235743.19534@rick.cs.ubc.ca>, h5346866@rick.cs.ubc.ca (bradley don head) writes:
> Questions:  How BIG (physically) is this classic? Does it require cooling (other
> than room A/C)?  What are its power requirements (i.e. 110V or 220V)?  Does the 11/83 usu. have SCSI
> interface(s)? If not, are there SCSI interface avail (3rd party)? How about 
> ethernet? How about memory expansion? Can we stuff 8Mb into it?
> The donator didn't (for whatever reasons) have the answers to these questions.
> I would appreciate some knowledgeable insite..

  The 11/83 comes in 2 major configuration styles: BA23 and BA123. The BA123
is a larger box (more expansion and drive capacity). These are floor-standing
units, although the BA23 can be rackmounted.

  It's not really "classic" as DEC was still selling them in late 1990 (and
probably still is). It doesn't need a raised floor, room cooling is fine. It
probably has a 120 Volt 15 Amp plug on it (regular wall socket). There is a
220 model, but only for export.

  SCSI is not native to any DEC PDP-11 system, although many vendors produce
adapters which work quite nicely. Ethernet is available as well. You can get
either a DEQNA (older, slower) or DELQA (newer, faster) from DEC. You can have
up to 4 Mb of memory in the system. It goes in semi-dedicated slots in the box.

  You can run (licenses permitting) DEC's RT-11, RSX, RSTS/E, or DSM-11 oper-
ating systems on it, as well as TSX (an RT-11 add-on for multi-users) and 2
BSD Unix, both available outside of DEC.

  If you decide you don't want it, let me know and we'll take it 8-). Alter-
natively, I can direct you to sites in Canada that would like it.

